it's great to be joined today by Anthony  scuze who is an Australia based  political scientist blogger and also  author of politics for the new Dark Age  staying positive amidst disorder which  we will get to Anthony so as you know I  came across your article about the  intellectual dark web an article will  act which actually happened to quote me  but that's not why you're actually on  the program it was a great article  regardless of that just so everybody  knows and what I found interesting about  your critique was that it matched up  with one particular element of my  critique of the intellectual dark web  which is that there's a lot of  rebranding and packaging and optics at  play for ideas that are not only not  unique but in some cases genuinely bad  or debunked ideas so let's maybe start  there what do you see as sort of the  crux of this movement that is the  so-called intellectual dark web  thanks David I think that's a really  interesting question and I the reason  why I wrote about this group was to try  and figure about out about what made  them distinctive and what made them  intellectually interesting if anything  and I think when you look at the merits  of it exactly as you say a lot of the  ideas that they're promoting very old  ideas from within the more conservative  side of liberalism and the libertarian  tradition and really ideas that have  been discussed and debated for many  years and I don't think the the free  speech warrior sort of narrative is is  very helpful either because empirically  of course these people extremely well  heard they have massive audiences and so  to other people that are protesting them  the people that challenge they have used  the people that critique they're at  their opinions and I think overall  institutions and society as a whole has  well-functioning institutional  mechanisms that are sort of on the whole  can be improved but overall able to  resolve these disputes about who gets to  speak and who gets to speak where and  when to sort of the satisfaction of  everybody involved so the thesis that I  develop in the blog article that you  came across is that the right that the  intellectual dark web is asserting is  not  they're right to free speech at all but  rather the right to protect their  reputation to the right to their good  names to their honor and their prestige  and that they're asserting that when  there's a conflict between their good  name between their reputation and the  free speech of others that their right  to their good name should ultimately  prevail  and this is I think what explains the  asymmetry between these you know this  this group of academics and media  personalities and their critics is that  our student protesters don't have you  know socially meaningful reputations  that can be infringed often minority  groups because of their perceived lower  social rank are used to having the you  know the culture and their social  behaviors critiqued in a way that  doesn't you know involve besmirching the  honor of the group as a whole  whereas I think the intellectual darkweb  the academics peterson harris and these  science types of people are asserting  that their reputations are worth  protecting and that they're claiming a  right which you know men and women of  their class and their social status and  let's be honest their race have been  able to assert for centuries and that is  to hold and express any of you in public  whatsoever without suffering any adverse  reputational consequences yeah and that  gets to this confusion that exists in a  lot of places between the fact that one  has a right to speak versus that they  are not exempt from the consequences of  that speech and maybe we'll get to that  one of the things I wanted to run by you  is that you know one of the under the  guise of this free speech and a battle  of ideas marketplace of ideas kind of  meme that has developed from the  intellectual dark web  I actually identify that many of the  positions that they are sort of claiming  are either heavily attacked positions  stigmatized positions positions which  there are authoritarian anti-free speech  elements that are really opposed to  they're not really positions that are  actually that controversial in other  words myself as like a progressive call  me whatever you want ready progressive  Social Democrat  in favor of you know equality feminism  and egalitarian it whatever right  characterize me however I agree with a  lot of the basics of what the so called  intellectual dark web is fighting for  which is there shouldn't really be taboo  subjects which can't be discussed it  should be okay to point out when one  religious group or another has ideas  that we might consider counter to the  best interests of society like these  things aren't even really that  controversial it's more the idea that  they've created an atmosphere where they  claim that it's not okay to talk about  this stuff when for the most part it's  fine to talk about this stuff yeah  that's exactly right and that really  gets back to the sort of the lack of  intellectual novelty of anything that  these characters are saying you know  it's been this all ultimately goes back  to a question of inequality and what  does inequality mean under a sort of a  liberal social order in which sort of  everyone is theoretically entitled to  the same rights and equal dignity and I  think what the intellectual darkweb and  sort of their intellectual predecessors  are really asserting is more or less a  standard set of positions sort of  right-wing liberalism has come to over  the last 50 years ever since Hayek and  Nozik and very Marie and rothbard and  that is a mix of libertarianism I this  idea that liberalism is ultimately all  about choice and that we judge  inequality based on the basis of whether  or not it respects that choice as well  as often a sort of consequentialist or  utilitarian logic which argues it well  inequality is not necessarily good but  sometimes it can be justified for the  good of the economy as a whole  and beyond that I I don't hear Harris or  Mari or pink sorry Harris or Pinker or  Jordan Peterson asserting any  perspective that is anything more  controversial than the standard  positions that are taught in  universities all all around the Western  world I was recently reading an article  that sought to sort of discuss what  environmental circumstances lead to a  rise in what the article called Charla  ten ISM and it pointed out interestingly  that for example when we started to see  modern medicine develop but before we  really knew  like what's quackery and what's real  modern medicine it gave rise to a ton of  so-called snake oil salesmen people  selling all sorts of you know liquids  herbs whatever right and that because  there was a very rapid increase in the  amount of information that there was to  read about or find out about because of  this acceleration of medical  circumstances it actually made it  possible for the charlatans to sort of  get in there because people are less  able to distinguish between all of this  new stuff that's available and thus at  the same time that there was like modern  medicine being developed there was a lot  of quackery that was perceived as maybe  equally valid is there an analogy from  that to what we're seeing now linking  sort of for example like the Trump  presidency and this post truth  alternative facts era and the pickup the  stickiness of the intellectual dark web  yeah I think it's actually an excellent  analogy and if you don't mind I just  want to sort of step back and sort of  explain how I see this fitting into sort  of a broader research area my sort of  general intellectual interest is in  culture as an evolutionary system so  this idea that cultural norms and  behavior evolve and change in accordance  with the mathematical laws of natural  selection so that the area's behavioral  mutation which leads to novel behaviors  and novel strategies some of those  strategies are more successful than  others and are therefore differentially  or preferentially copied in the next  generation now when it comes to humans  as a social species one of the main way  in which people learn new behaviors and  gain new information is by imitation we  copy what other people do because on the  whole it's more efficient when it room  to do as the Romans do rather than  trying to use our brains and sort of tax  our intellectual resources to come up  with the most efficient efficient social  strategy on our the problem is imitation  creates its own set of social dilemmas  the question is who do we copy who is  worthy of imitation who has the best  information whose theory of the case is  the most scientifically informed or the  best best tested by evidence and exactly  as you pointed out there's often an  advantage for people to have their  strategy copied and they may have  incentives to deceive about the  reliability of the evidence on which  their theory is based or the  effectiveness of the strategies that  there are commit that they're  recommending so the theory of who do we  copy from and who is worthy of imitation  and who is worthy of learning is really  important to understanding how cultural  evolution works and there are four basic  mechanisms that sort of mathematically  can be shown to work in this regard  they're sort of cute trusts based on kid  networks I I trust and copy you because  you're my relative there's Network  reciprocity where I trust or I copy your  behavior because we are physically  proximate to one another  there's record reputation mechanisms  basically I copy your behavior because  your high status person in my society  and I know that you have been successful  in the past and the fourth and final one  is is group identity I the behavior of  other people and my identity group  because that group enos is what makes us  successful and so looking at reputation  and the importance of reputation in  terms of social reproduction and how  ideas spread throughout the societies I  think vastly important because although  reputation isn't important in one  societies in a lot of ways I would argue  that in the culture industry where the  intellectual darkweb primarily comes  from places like academia places like  media and social media in particular  reputation is still very important and  it is important to academics it is  important to media personalities that  they be seen and perceived to have high  level levels of prestige and high levels  of trust because that's the way that  they ensure that their ideas are  propagated in the limited time we have  left I do want to touch on the book a  little bit I regularly hear from people  who are of two mindsets right now in  this sort of current world of politics  one is they're more engaged than ever  because they feel a more acute need to  try to get in the way of what they see  is  really negative turns that society and  culture have taken others though are  opting to opt out altogether because  it's just so difficult and horrifying  and depressing and they're saying I'm  just gonna stop paying attention to  politics and hope I kind of get through  my life in an okay way what would be  your approach in terms of how to be  engaged in a way that's maybe not  depressing or whatever word we want to  apply to it I I think you can probably  tell from my use of evolutionary  paradigms and I I'm fairly comfortable  with conflict I you know I'm used to  thinking of politics and of culture as  conflictual systems and sort of the  optimistic way of looking at their  systems this is say yes it's brutal  yes it's often competitive you know  often the social equilibrium that occur  and not the social equal equilibria that  we would want but over the long arc of  human history you do see social progress  come out of their systems because  evolution is a learning system that does  increase the complexity of information  it does increase the level of  cooperativeness and the scale at which  human societies can organize so if  you're looking for a positive message  about how far we've come as a species  and and what we can achieve I think you  know thinking of society and politics as  conflictual is is not necessarily a net  negative and so I wrote the book to  really provide a sort of a step-by-step  way of sort of leading people from that  view of politics as conflictual and  showing sort of moving from issue to  issue how you can go from that to sort  of seeing trust and social cooperation  as the basis of the politics that we  need for the future the book is politics  for the new Dark Age staying positive  amidst disorder we're linking to it in  the description for the YouTube video  we've been speaking with the book's  author Anthony Skewes who's an Australia  based political scientist blogger and  author Anthony thank you so much for  talking to us today thanks very much  David it was pleasure

